course: dictionary
lesson: 1312
title: One word, two meanings
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the sentence tells you which one is meant. The dictionary gives two meanings for cadet. «Kieran joined the cadets, where he learned rules, teamwork and how to march in a straight line» shows a group where children learn skills, so this is the second meaning.
explain: The first meaning is a young person training to be an officer in the army, navy, air force or police. Look for clues such as training, officer or uniform. Ask yourself: does the sentence sound like a club, or like training for a job?
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. «Clubs and teams, like football or cadets, give energy and toughness a useful, safe place to go.» Cadets sits in a list next to football, so it is a group you can join, which means the second meaning fits.
fact: A cadet is a young person in training, such as a trainee officer in the army, navy, air force or police.
fact: In a youth club, a cadet is a child or teenager who joins Army Cadets, Sea Cadets or Air Cadets.
fact: Cadet groups teach skills, drill and teamwork.
fact: «Police cadet», «air cadets», «cadet force», «naval cadet» and «join the cadets» are common phrases.
fact: Some words have more than one meaning, so always check the sentence to find the right one.
exercise: «Kieran joined the cadets, where he learned rules, teamwork and how to march.» What kind of group are the cadets? | *A youth club linked to the armed forces | A group of pilots who fly planes | A school for future doctors | A football team that wins every match
exercise: What is a «police cadet»? | A car used by the police | *A young person training to join the police | A police station in a small town | A police officer who has retired
exercise: «Clubs and teams, like football or cadets, give energy and toughness a useful, safe place to go.» Find and copy one word from the sentence that names a group children can join. | energy | toughness | useful | *cadets
test: Ruby says a cadet must already be a grown-up officer. Is she right? | No, cadets only play football | Yes, cadets never learn anything | *No, a cadet is a young person in training | Yes, cadets are always grown-up officers
test: «He wanted to join the cadets, so he went to the first meeting.» What does join the cadets mean? | Buy a new uniform in a shop | *Become a member of a youth group linked to the armed forces | Go to school in the army | Stop playing sport forever
exam: Which sentence uses cadet to mean a child in a youth club? | The cadet was a famous singer. | The cadet climbed the mountain alone. | *The cadets learned how to march in a straight line. | The cadet sold ice cream at the beach.
